  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Use mathematical induction to prove each of the following:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q6a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$5^n-1$ is divisible by $4$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nStart at $n=1$ and use $5^k-1=4m$ to obtain $5^{k+1}-1=4(5m+1)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: $5^1-1=4$, a multiple of $4$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $5^k-1=4m$ for some integer $m$, where $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}5^{k+1}-1&=5\\cdot5^k-1\\\\5^{k+1}-1&=5(4m+1)-1\\\\5^{k+1}-1&=20m+4\\\\5^{k+1}-1&=4(5m+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $4$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q6b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$7^n-4^n$ is divisible by $3$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse $7^k-4^k=3m$ to obtain $7^{k+1}-4^{k+1}=3(7m+4^k)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: $7^1-4^1=3$, a multiple of $3$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $7^k-4^k=3m$ for some integer $m$, where $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}7^{k+1}-4^{k+1}&=7\\cdot7^k-4\\cdot4^k\\\\7^{k+1}-4^{k+1}&=7(3m+4^k)-4\\cdot4^k\\\\7^{k+1}-4^{k+1}&=21m+3\\cdot4^k\\\\7^{k+1}-4^{k+1}&=3(7m+4^k).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $3$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q6c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$n^3+2n$ is divisible by $3$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse $k^3+2k=3m$ to obtain $(k+1)^3+2(k+1)=3(m+k^2+k+1)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: $1^3+2\\cdot1=3$, a multiple of $3$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $k^3+2k=3m$ for some integer $m$, where $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}(k+1)^3+2(k+1)&=k^3+3k^2+5k+3\\\\(k+1)^3+2(k+1)&=(k^3+2k)+3k^2+3k+3\\\\(k+1)^3+2(k+1)&=3m+3k^2+3k+3\\\\(k+1)^3+2(k+1)&=3(m+k^2+k+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $3$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q6d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$2^{2n-1}+1$ is divisible by $3$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse $2^{2k-1}+1=3m$ to obtain $2^{2(k+1)-1}+1=3(4m-1)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: $2^{2\\cdot1-1}+1=3$, a multiple of $3$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $2^{2k-1}+1=3m$ for some integer $m$, where $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}2^{2(k+1)-1}+1&=4\\cdot2^{2k-1}+1\\\\2^{2(k+1)-1}+1&=4(3m-1)+1\\\\2^{2(k+1)-1}+1&=12m-3\\\\2^{2(k+1)-1}+1&=3(4m-1).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $3$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q6e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$5^{2n-1}+2^{2n-1}$ is divisible by $7$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nUse $5^{2k-1}+2^{2k-1}=7m$ to obtain $5^{2(k+1)-1}+2^{2(k+1)-1}=7(25m-3\\cdot2^{2k-1})$.",
          "worked": "Base case: $5^{2\\cdot1-1}+2^{2\\cdot1-1}=5+2=7$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $5^{2k-1}+2^{2k-1}=7m$ for some integer $m$, where $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}5^{2(k+1)-1}+2^{2(k+1)-1}&=25\\cdot5^{2k-1}+4\\cdot2^{2k-1}\\\\5^{2(k+1)-1}+2^{2(k+1)-1}&=25(7m-2^{2k-1})+4\\cdot2^{2k-1}\\\\5^{2(k+1)-1}+2^{2(k+1)-1}&=175m-21\\cdot2^{2k-1}\\\\5^{2(k+1)-1}+2^{2(k+1)-1}&=7(25m-3\\cdot2^{2k-1}).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is a multiple of $7$. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the result holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 95
      }
    ]
  },
